.TH ASK 1 "2026-08-13" "ask 0.1.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
ask \- put a question through a language model
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ask
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ message ...]
.br
.B ask replay
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B ask compact
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ session ]
.br
.B ask note
.B \-s
.I source
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ text ...]
.br
.B ask system
.br
.B ask login
.I openai\-codex
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B ask logout
.I provider
.br
.B ask auth
.RI [ list ]
.br
.B ask version
.br
.B ask help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ask
sends a message to a language model and writes the answer to standard
output. It is a filter: the answer is stdout, progress is stderr, and the
exit status says what happened, so it composes with the rest of the shell.
.PP
There is no tool loop and no agent loop. The model answers, and the answer
is the product.
.PP
Every model turn appends to an append\-only JSONL session log. The conversation
sent to the model is a pure function of that log \(em each call is preceded
by a
.I request
event recording the normalized request fields, with the conversation itself
stored as a SHA\-256 digest rather than a second copy.
.B ask replay \-check
recomputes the fold and compares, which is what makes "replays exactly" a
claim that can fail rather than a slogan.
.PP
Anything that is not a command is a message. A word one edit away from a
command is refused as a probable typo rather than sent to the model;
.B \-\-
forces a word through as a message.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ask \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fImessage\fR...]
Ask. Arguments are joined with spaces. Piped or redirected standard input
becomes the message, or rides with one inside
.I <stdin>
delimiters when both are given. Surrounding whitespace on textual stdin is
removed. By default this starts a new conversation; see
.B \-c
and
.BR \-f
for explicit continuation.
.TP
.B ask replay \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIsession\fR]
Re\-render a session. The argument is a path or a bare session id; with
none, the current session. A damaged log renders as far as it reads and
exits 1;
.B \-check
refuses it outright, because what cannot be read cannot be proved.
.TP
.B ask compact \fR[\fIflags\fR] [\fIsession\fR]
Continue a conversation that filled its window, in a fresh one.
.IP
A session that overflows fails identically forever, which is what exit 2
means. Continuing requires less context. A model therefore writes a handoff
note, and
.B compact
records that operation explicitly.
.IP
Three files, three roles. The source is never touched. The summarizer runs
in a session of its own, so the call that wrote the note replays like any
other. The new session holds the note as its first message, stamped
.BR source=summary ,
with its
.B parent
and its
.B summary
session named in the header. Each of the three replays alone.
.IP
The new session lands beside the source.
.B current
moves only if the source was the current session: compacting the
conversation you are in moves you into the compact one, and compacting some
other file leaves you where you were. Standard output is the new session's
path, so it can be handed straight to
.BR \-f .
.IP
Reasoning is left out of the transcript because it is provider specific.
Attachment bytes are not carried; the transcript records their media type.
.IP
If the transcript does not fit either, that is exit 2 again, naming
.BR \-m :
compact with a wider window.
.IP
Branching a conversation verbatim needs no verb. A session is a
self\-contained file and
.B \-f
names one, so
.BR cp (1)
is fork.
.TP
.B ask note \fR\-s \fIsource\fR [\fIflags\fR] [\fItext ...\fR]
Record attributed text in a session. The text comes from the arguments, or
from standard input when there are none. Standard input is limited to 16 MB.
No model is called, so there is no request event and nothing new to replay.
.IP
.B \-s
is required, is one word, and names the program recording the note.
.IP
A note is not folded. It is a record, not a message, so it does not change
the conversation sent to a provider or any existing request digest.
.IP
For machine-readable evidence,
.BI \-k " kind"
names the record type,
.BI \-json " body"
supplies a JSON value (use
.B \-json \-
to read it from standard input), and
.B \-seal
fsyncs the record followed by a digest of the exact event prefix. All three
options are required together. Replay checks event numbering, that each
structured note is immediately sealed, and the seal digest. The seal detects
later edits; it is not a cryptographic identity signature.
Because the digest lives in the same append-only file, it is not external
proof that the file was never truncated to an earlier valid sealed prefix.
.TP
.B ask system
Print the built\-in system prompt on stdout. The prompt is a value, not a
secret, so extending it is ordinary shell rather than a config format:
.IP
.EX
ask \-S "$(ask system; cat house\-style.md)" "..."
.EE
.TP
.B ask login \fIopenai\-codex\fR [\fIflags\fR]
Store subscription credentials in the auth file.
.B \-from\-codex
imports them from the official Codex CLI and is the usual path.
.TP
.B ask logout \fIprovider\fR
Remove stored credentials for a provider.
.TP
.B ask auth \fR[\fIlist\fR]
List stored credential providers and the state of each token. The auth
file path goes to stderr, the entries to stdout.
.TP
.B ask version
Print the version.
.B \-V
and
.B \-\-version
do the same.
.TP
.B ask help
Print the usage summary.
.B \-h
and
.B \-\-help
do the same.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a " file"
Attach a file. Repeat for more, and they reach the model in the order
given. What a file is comes from reading it, never from its name: a text
file is inlined with a sanitized base name, and an image, PDF, audio or video
file is attached. See
.B ATTACHMENTS
below.
.TP
.BI \-m " provider/model"
Model to use, for example
.IR anthropic/your\-model .
Without
.BR \-m ,
.B $ASK_MODEL
wins when nonempty. Otherwise a continued session uses the model it was
started with. A new session with neither is an error.
OpenRouter models keep their own slash:
.IR openrouter/vendor/model .
.TP
.BI \-S " text"
System prompt for this invocation. Without
.BR \-S ,
.B $ASK_SYSTEM
is used when set, otherwise the built\-in prompt is used.
An explicitly empty
.B \-S ""
sends no system prompt at all. The choice is made for each invocation; a
prompt passed when a session was created is not inherited by later calls.
.TP
.B \-c
Continue the session named by the conversation directory's
.I current
symlink. A missing or dangling pointer is an error; no other session is
chosen as a fallback. This cannot be combined with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read and append to this session log, keeping a thread wherever the caller
wants it. An existing file is continued; a nonexistent file is created.
Other errors inspecting the path are reported. This never consults or
changes the conversation directory's current pointer.
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
Conversation directory. Defaults to
.BR $ASK_DIR ,
else
.IR ~/.ask/sessions .
.TP
.BI \-effort " level"
Reasoning effort:
.IR off ,
.IR low ,
.IR medium ,
.IR high ,
or
.IR xhigh .
The default leaves the effort level to the provider. OpenAI and OpenRouter
receive explicit
.IR low ,
.IR medium ,
.IR high ,
and
.I xhigh
levels. Anthropic and Gemini do not distinguish those levels; each
enables the same thinking configuration. For OpenRouter,
.I off
omits the reasoning parameter rather than sending an explicit disable value.
.TP
.BI \-max\-tokens " n"
Maximum output tokens for the answer. Default 16384 on adapters that support
it. An answer cut off at this limit is reported on stderr and still exits 0
\(em it is output, just
incomplete. With
.BR \-schema ,
a cut off document exits 1 and stdout stays empty.
.B openai\-codex
does not support this option; explicitly setting it is an error, and when it
is omitted no output limit is sent.
.TP
.BI \-schema " file"
Constrain the answer with the self\-contained JSON Schema in
.IR file .
A file name of
.B \-
reads the schema from standard input; the question must then come from the
arguments.
.TP
.B \-json
Emit the raw events written by this invocation on stdout instead of the
answer.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress progress on stderr. Errors still go to stderr.
.TP
.B \-check
.RB ( replay )
Verify the replay invariant before producing output.
Combined with
.BR \-json ,
emit the exact verified event snapshot as JSONL.
.TP
.BI \-step " n"
.RB ( replay )
Print the normalized model request at this sequence number, with the
conversation reconstituted from the fold its digest stands for. Transport
routing fields and provider wire transformations are not part of this view.
.PP
.B compact
takes
.BR \-m ,
.B \-d
and
.B \-q
with their usual meanings, except that
.B \-m
names the model that writes the handoff note and defaults to the one the
source session recorded, not to
.BR $ASK_MODEL .
.PP
.B note
takes
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-d ,
and
.BR \-q .
.B \-s
is required and must be one word.
.SS Login options
.TP
.B \-from\-codex
Import a ChatGPT login from the official Codex CLI auth file. It cannot be
combined with token flags.
.TP
.BI \-access\-token " token"
Store an access token. A value of
.B \-
reads it from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-refresh\-token " token"
Store a refresh token. A value of
.B \-
reads it from standard input. Only one token field may read stdin.
.TP
.BI \-token\-url " url"
OAuth token endpoint used to refresh a stored token. It must use HTTPS,
except on loopback.
.TP
.BI \-client\-id " id"
OAuth client id sent during refresh.
.TP
.BI \-scope " scope"
OAuth scope sent during refresh.
.TP
.BI \-expires " duration"
Lifetime of the supplied access token, in Go duration syntax such as
.IR 1h .
.SH STRUCTURED OUTPUT
.B \-schema
passes a JSON Schema through the provider's native structured\-output field.
It does not put the schema in the system prompt or user message. The schema
is recorded on the request event, beside the other normalized request
fields, so
.B replay \-step
reconstitutes it and
.B replay \-check
keeps making the same assertion about the conversation.
.PP
After streaming, the answer is parsed and validated locally. Invalid JSON,
a schema mismatch, a refusal, or a response cut off at the output limit is
an error and writes no normal answer to stdout. The provider turn remains in
the append\-only log, followed by a
.B done
event naming the error.
With
.BR \-json ,
stdout still carries the requested raw event stream.
.PP
Schemas are at most 1 MB and must be one JSON object. Definitions may use
fragment references within that object; external references are refused.
These checks happen before a new session file is created. Provider support
is model dependent: an unsupported model returns a provider error rather
than falling back to prompt instructions. OpenRouter is restricted to
endpoints that accept the structured\-output parameters.
.PP
.EX
ask \-schema result.schema.json "extract the incident severity" < alert.txt
.EE
.SH ATTACHMENTS
.B \-a
attaches a file, and repeats:
.PP
.EX
ask \-a chart.png "what is the trend?"
ask \-a q3.pdf \-a q4.pdf "what changed between these quarters?"
.EE
.PP
The path must name a nonempty regular file.
.B \-a \-
is refused; pipe the data on standard input instead.
.PP
What a file is comes from its content. A name is a name, not a type: a
.I .png
holding a shell script is a shell script, and a
.I .txt
holding a PNG is an image. A file whose bytes carry a recognised signature
is that type; anything else that is valid UTF\-8 without NUL bytes is text;
anything else is refused, by name, before it is sent.
.PP
Text files are inlined, labelled with their base name \(em
.B \-a main.go
is
.B cat main.go
with a tag \(em so a session log stays something
.BR grep (1)
and
.BR jq (1)
can read. Images, PDFs, audio and video are attached as bytes.
.PP
Binary arriving on standard input is an attachment too, so no flag is
needed for the common case:
.PP
.EX
screencapture \-x \-t png \- \(bv ask "what is on my screen?"
.EE
.PP
Text on standard input keeps its old meaning and rides inside the message
as evidence.
.SS What each provider carries
.TS
tab(:);
l l.
anthropic:images, PDF
openai, openai\-codex:images, PDF
gemini:images, audio, video, PDF
openrouter:images, PDF, WAV and MP3
.TE
.PP
An attachment a provider cannot carry is refused before the session log is
touched, naming the provider and what it does take. This is checked per
provider, not per model: a model that is merely the wrong model for the job
is the provider's own error to report.
.SS Limits
The
.B \-a
flags accept at most 16 files, 16 MB each and 32 MB in total. These bounds
are fixed because the bytes are read whole, encoded into the session log,
and encoded again into the request.
.PP
Standard input has its own 16 MB bound, and crossing it is an error
rather than a truncation. A filter that quietly dropped the tail of its
input would answer a question about the first part of a file and present it
as an answer about the file, and nothing downstream could tell the
difference. In practice the model's context window is the real ceiling and
it is far lower; see
.B EXIT STATUS
2.
.PP
The bytes live in the session log. A photograph makes a large JSONL record,
and the session is correspondingly large; in exchange the log remains one
self\-contained file that still replays exactly, and copying it still copies
everything it means. Session files are created mode 0600.
.SH CONVERSATIONS
A session is one JSONL file in the conversation directory, named by a
sortable id. The
.I current
symlink names the session a bare
.B ask
most recently started. It is followed only when
.B \-c
is explicit. A session selected with
.B \-f
never changes
.IR current .
.PP
Because a plain invocation starts fresh, independent pipelines do not inherit
conversation state. Use
.B \-c
for the current conversation or
.B \-f
for a named thread.
Every run that continues a session announces which one it joined and how
many turns it already holds, unless
.B \-q
suppresses progress.
.PP
Only one process may write to a session at a time; a second is refused.
Two writers would interleave two conversations into one file, and every
digest written afterwards would fail its fold. The lock is an exclusive
.BR flock (2)
on the session file, so the kernel releases it when the writer exits
however it exits: a session is never stranded by a crash, and there is no
lock file to find or delete.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ASK_MODEL
Default for
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B ASK_SYSTEM
Default system prompt, overriding the built\-in one and overridden by
.BR \-S .
.TP
.B ASK_DIR
Default for
.BR \-d .
.TP
.B ASK_AUTH_FILE
Path to the stored credential file. Default
.IR ~/.ask/auth.json ,
created with mode 0600.
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\fR, \fBOPENAI_API_KEY\fR, \fBGEMINI_API_KEY\fR, \fBOPENROUTER_API_KEY
Vendor API keys. Each is required only for the provider that uses it, and
becomes optional behind a gateway or on Vertex, where the credential lives
in the transport.
.TP
.BI < PROVIDER >_BASE_URL
Replace a provider endpoint. The exact names are
.BR ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL ,
.BR OPENAI_BASE_URL ,
.BR OPENAI_CODEX_BASE_URL ,
.BR GEMINI_BASE_URL ,
and
.BR OPENROUTER_BASE_URL .
.TP
.B ASK_AUTH_URL
OAuth token endpoint for a gateway. Its presence enables bearer
authentication for Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, and OpenRouter, including
Anthropic requests using the Vertex wire format. The
.B openai\-codex
adapter continues to use its stored subscription credential.
.B ASK_AUTH_CLIENT_ID
and
.B ASK_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET
are sent as client_secret_post form fields;
.B ASK_AUTH_REFRESH_TOKEN
selects the refresh_token grant instead of client_credentials; and
.B ASK_AUTH_SCOPE
is sent when set. Tokens are held in memory for the life of the process
and never written to disk.
.IP
The endpoint must be
.BR https ,
except on loopback. The request body carries a client secret or a refresh
token, so http would put the long\-lived half of the credential on the wire
in the clear; a gateway inside a corporate network is still reached across
a network. The same rule applies to
.B \-token\-url
at
.B ask login
and to a
.I token_url
already stored in the credential file, and it is checked when the endpoint
is configured rather than only when a token expires.
.TP
.B ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_PROJECT_ID
Route
.I anthropic/
models through Google Vertex AI. Its presence enables Vertex, and
.B CLOUD_ML_REGION
is then required \(em
.IR global ,
.IR us ,
.IR eu ,
or a specific region such as
.IR us\-east5 .
.B ANTHROPIC_VERTEX_BASE_URL
replaces the region\-derived endpoint.
.B ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL
is ignored while Vertex is enabled.
.IP
.B ask
does not obtain Google credentials. Use
.B ASK_AUTH_URL
or a proxy that adds authentication.
.TP
.B CODEX_HOME
Codex CLI directory used by
.BR "ask login openai-codex -from-codex" .
The default is
.IR ~/.codex .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour on stderr. Colour is used only when stderr is a terminal.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.ask/sessions/
Session logs, one JSONL file per conversation, plus the
.I current
symlink. Directories created by
.B ask
use mode 0700; session files use mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.ask/auth.json
Stored credentials, created with mode 0600. The path is replaced by
.BR $ASK_AUTH_FILE
when set.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully. For an ask turn, the model answered.
.TP
.B 1
An error: bad usage, a missing key, a provider failure, a corrupt log, or
a turn that produced no text. Structured output also uses exit 1 for invalid
JSON, a schema mismatch, a refusal, or an incomplete stop.
.TP
.B 2
The context window is full. This is permanent for that conversation \(em
the same request will fail identically forever \(em so it is told apart
from an ordinary error, and a retry loop can stop instead of grinding.
Start a new conversation by omitting
.B \-c
and
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. Any blocks already streamed are logged as a partial turn and
excluded from context. An
.B abort
event records the interruption, so the session can be continued and the
question asked again.
.SH EXAMPLES
Ask a question:
.PP
.EX
ask what is a monad
.EE
.PP
Use a pipe as the data and the argument as the instruction:
.PP
.EX
git diff \(bv ask "write a commit message"
.EE
.PP
Continue one conversation, then start another:
.PP
.EX
ask "what is a monad"
ask \-c "give me an example in Go"
ask "unrelated question"
.EE
.PP
Keep a named thread and verify it later:
.PP
.EX
ask \-f review.jsonl "review this diff" < patch
ask \-f review.jsonl "summarize the risks"
ask replay \-check review.jsonl
.EE
.PP
Require JSON that another program can test:
.PP
.EX
ask \-schema decision.schema.json "classify this alert" < alert.txt \
  \(bv jq \-e '.urgent'
.EE
.PP
Attach two files in order:
.PP
.EX
ask \-a before.png \-a after.png "list what changed"
.EE
.PP
Add an attributed record without calling a model:
.PP
.EX
ask note \-s test \-f review.jsonl "go test ./... passed"
.EE
.PP
Continue a full conversation from an explicit handoff:
.PP
.EX
new=$(ask compact)
ask \-f "$new" "continue with the next case"
.EE
.PP
Branch on the answer, not merely on exit 0:
.PP
.EX
if ask \-q "does this log show an OOM? answer yes or no" < kern.log \
  \(bv grep \-Eiq '^yes$'; then echo OOM; fi
.EE
.PP
Prove every session on disk still replays:
.PP
.EX
for f in ~/.ask/sessions/*.jsonl; do ask replay \-check "$f" || echo "$f"; done
.EE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Progress, reasoning, retries, errors and the closing token count go to stderr,
so
.B 2>/dev/null
leaves the answer alone. Reasoning renders dimmed when stderr is a
terminal. When stdout and stderr are the same file or terminal, the answer
is not printed twice. With
.BR \-q ,
progress is suppressed but errors still print.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
.BR sh (1)
.SH CAVEATS
A word one edit from a command is refused rather than sent to the model.
This is deliberate \(em a typo would otherwise be a paid model call
appended to the live conversation \(em but it means
.B ask verison
must be written
.B ask \-\- verison
to reach the model as a message.
.PP
Switching providers mid\-conversation is allowed and degrades gracefully:
each adapter replays its own opaque reasoning state and ignores another's.
The text of the conversation always survives; the thinking behind it may
not.
